I have learned a little C, in a college class, years ago. Modula 2
long before that, and self taught basic in my teens. The most advanced
stuff I ever wrote was probably the earliest, pushing pixels about on
my Amiga to make bitmap-based scrolltexts and the like. I think I even
coded a functioning "Tron" game once, that worked by looking at the
colour of the pixels on screen to detect collisions. I didn't know any
better.

All forgotten now, of course, except for an amateurish (and often
incorrect, no doubt) idea of how programs work and are structured. I
found the code itself was easy enough to get on with for really basic
stuff, but stuff like compiling, knowing what is in each library and
how to find out, and the entire structure of the damn thing (all.
the .h and .c files and build files and everything in C- what the hell
is all that about?) to be more than a little unwieldy.

To me it felt a lot like the experiences I've had since with Linux. (I
am a happy Ubuntu user, mind you.) It's all there for you to learn and
use and take apart and put back together, but everything is just
creaking with lumpy old legacies that you're expected to *just know*.
As soon as you open it up you're presented with this whole mess of
stuff, and you have to know all of it. You can't just dip in and learn
one little bit, and then learn the next bit and build up your
knowledge like that. And as soon as you ask someone how something
works, you never get a simple answer:
"How do I do X?"
"Well, first you have to understand that X is the way it is because of
Y, which was first implemented about 10 years ago, when it replaced Z,
which used to work like this.... (and on and on for about 2 hours)
"Yes, but all I want to do is X."
"Oh, OK, just type in this:"
*types it in*
"I did that, it didn't work."
"Oh, that's strange. Maybe you have V on your system."
"I don't know, what is V?"
"Well, to understand V you have to understand U, which isn't used any
more, but was better than T, which..."

I guess nethack must seem equally obscure to outsiders, but at least
it has the advantage of being a metaphor for familiar real-world
concepts (hunger, fighting, fire, water, moving around a map etc).
Imagine nethack with all the natural language stripped out, totally
abstracted (or unabstracted) into a non-real-world framework where
XNAHT DUAM just means "XNAHT DUAM" instead of "enchant armour" and a
sword is just a ) that you w and then 12346789 or F into a D or a h or
a k, so that it just becomes a blind and random navigation around an
inexplicably complex set of arbitrary rules. Imagine that, and you
have an idea of how I feel about Linux and C programming.

By the way, I'm no luddite: I work in IT. I understand computers and
spend a lot of time  tinkering with them/ breaking them/ fixing them,
but some aspects of computing I am more suited to than others.

